From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mikhail Zinchenko <m1d3ry@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Use char16_t for UTF-16 strings under libc++ on Windows

On Windows the SDK spells UTF-16 as `unsigned short`. That works around a Visual Studio 2015 link error
on `std::codecvt<char16_t, char, mbstate_t>::id` in the Microsoft STL. The workaround relies on the
generic `std::char_traits` primary template, which only the Microsoft STL supplies. libc++ specializes
`char_traits` for the standard character types alone, so `std::basic_string<unsigned short>` and
`std::codecvt_utf8_utf16<unsigned short>` fail to compile. clang-cl with libc++ hits this.

libc++ has no export bug for `char16_t`, so use the portable spelling there. The check keys on
`_LIBCPP_VERSION`, which `<string>` defines before the aliases. Builds with the Microsoft STL are
unchanged.

The change stays local. `StringType` and `StringConvert` appear only in `UnicodeConversion.cpp`, and
`utf8_to_u16string` copies the result into a `std::u16string` either way.
---
 src/inc/internal/UnicodeConversion.hpp | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/inc/internal/UnicodeConversion.hpp b/src/inc/internal/UnicodeConversion.hpp
index 48cd9a30..e437457e 100644
--- a/src/inc/internal/UnicodeConversion.hpp
+++ b/src/inc/internal/UnicodeConversion.hpp
@@ -24,7 +24,10 @@ namespace MSIX {
         There is also a similar error to this one with 'char16_t' that can be worked around using 'unsigned short'.
         <snip>
     */
-    #ifdef WIN32
+    // The workaround above is for the Microsoft STL only. libc++ defines std::char_traits for the standard
+    // character types alone, so std::basic_string<unsigned short> does not compile there, and libc++ has no
+    // codecvt<char16_t> export bug. _LIBCPP_VERSION comes from <string> above.
+    #if defined(WIN32) && !defined(_LIBCPP_VERSION)
     using StringType = std::basic_string<unsigned short>;
     using StringConvert = std::wstring_convert<std::codecvt_utf8_utf16<unsigned short>, unsigned short>;
     #else
